course: dictionary
lesson: 14070
title: The word maw
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A maw is the mouth, jaws or throat of a hungry or fierce animal. Picture a wolf with its jaws wide open. You can often work out a new word by looking at the words near it. «The dragon opened its gaping maw and roared.» Gaping means wide open, and roared tells you the dragon is fierce, so maw must mean its wide, open mouth.
explain: A maw can also be any dark opening that seems to swallow things up. «The torch beam did not reach the maw of the cave.» The cave is dark and deep, and the light cannot get into it, so maw here means the dark opening of the cave, not a mouth at all.
explain: In Scotland, maw is an informal word for mum. «"Maw, my tea's ready!" shouted Callum.» Paw is the Scottish word for dad. Always check the sentence: is the word talking about an animal, a dark hole, or a person in the family?
fact: A maw is the mouth, jaws or throat of a hungry or fierce animal.
fact: «Maw» can mean a dark opening that seems to swallow things up, like «the maw of the cave».
fact: «Gaping maw» is a phrase that means a very wide, open mouth.
fact: In Scotland, «maw» (informal) means mum, and «paw» means dad.
fact: The word maw often makes a creature or place sound scary, greedy or dangerous.
exercise: «The dragon opened its gaping maw and roared.» What is the maw? | A cave in the hills | The dragon's long tail | *The wide mouth of the dragon
exercise: In Scotland, Callum shouts, «Maw, my tea's ready!» Who is Maw? | A dragon | *Callum's mum | Callum's dog | Callum's dad
exercise: «The torch beam did not reach the maw of the cave.» What does maw mean here? | The roof of the cave | *The dark opening that seems to swallow things up | The sound of water | A pile of rocks
test: «The wolf showed its sharp teeth in its open maw.» What part of the wolf is its maw? | A hard shell | Its wings | *Its jaws and throat | Its thick fur
test: «Scared, Tom stepped into the maw of the dark tunnel.» What does the phrase «into the maw of» suggest? | Sitting down for a rest | *Going straight into something that seems to swallow things up | Stepping out into the sunshine | Climbing up a tall tree
exam: Which sentence uses maw to mean a dark opening that seems to swallow things up? | *«The maw of the tunnel swallowed the last glimmer of light.» | «Maw, can I have a biscuit?» | «Paw came home late from work.» | «Our class went to the zoo today.»
